WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.140
A detection use case defines a security-relevant condition, why it matters, the evidence expected, and the action an analyst should take.

2
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:18.680
In OT, use cases should be prioritized by credible operational consequence and available visibility rather than by a generic list of alert ideas.

3
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:26.880
Unauthorized remote access can combine identity, gateway, approval, session, and destination evidence.

4
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:34.340
Useful logic asks whether the account, time, source, target, and access path are expected.

5
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:43.080
An unexpected communication path may indicate a segmentation problem, temporary maintenance route, configuration error, or security event.

6
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.160
New or unknown devices deserve context.

7
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:54.500
A passive sensor may observe a replacement controller, vendor laptop, unmanaged switch, or unauthorized asset.

8
00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:03.240
Detection should identify the affected zone and normal peers, then route validation to the asset owner rather than declaring every new address hostile.

9
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.520
Engineering-workstation misuse and unusual controller interaction are high-value cases.

10
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:19.540
Evidence may include access outside a maintenance window, connection to an unexpected controller, new project activity, mode change, or an unauthorized configuration record.

11
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:25.560
Correlation with work orders and approved personnel reduces ambiguity.

12
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:32.000
Protocol-aware detections can identify unusual writes, commands, sessions, or role changes.

13
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.340
Endpoint telemetry may reveal suspicious files or removable-media activity near privileged engineering tools.

14
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:44.440
These observations require careful triage because legitimate support work can produce similar signals.

15
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:53.600
Each use case should document data dependencies, exclusions, severity factors, owner, process context, and a safe response path.

16
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.220
The objective is not to label technical events automatically.

17
00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:05.560
It is to produce evidence-rich alerts that help security, operations, and engineering reach a timely, defensible decision.
